# Hero Room, WEATHER EDITION — the exhibits on an open GRID PLANE, with rain and a real bow.
#
# The same nine hero pieces, in the same places, as scenes/gallery_settled.ftsl (which stays
# the canonical still + flyby scene; this is a separate edition, not a replacement — it is a
# copy of that file, so re-bake it from gallery.ftsl the same way if the pieces ever move),
# plus a TENTH that exists only here: the CRYSTAL AXICON, front-left, which is the only piece
# in either scene that throws a genuinely COLOURED caustic. See its own block down in HERO
# PIECES for the measurements that chose its shape, angle and height.
# What is different here is the weather, and the setting built to make the weather legible:
#
#   * NO ROOM. The walls, the ceiling and the polished stone floor are gone. In their place
#     is one wide, near-black glossy GROUND PLANE ruled by a glowing green wireframe GRID,
#     under a black sky — so the whole hemisphere above the exhibits is open and every piece
#     is lit by the full sun instead of by a slot in a roof. That is the point of the change:
#     the enclosed hall left the left half of the frame in permanent shadow.
#   * The museum STANDS follow the ground: their base and column are green wireframe CAGES,
#     but each keeps a SOLID WHITE CAP. The cap is not decoration — it is the screen the
#     dispersive caustics from the glass sphere and the diamond gyroid land on, and a
#     wireframe would have thrown that away (a cage catches almost nothing).
#   * the scene's ONE cloud, in the back-right, shaped by the actual cloud1.glb MESH rather
#     than by an algebraic blob — `medium { bounds { object … } }` solid-voxelizes the mesh
#     into an occupancy lattice, `feather 0.13` softens that lattice's silhouette into a
#     falloff zone the way a real cloud edge is, and `mesh { shape_only yes }` throws the
#     1.85 M triangles away once the bake has read them so the cloud is fog and nothing else
#     (see FTSL 8.4/12.1);
#   * a RAIN curtain falling out of it — starting INSIDE it, not below it — carrying
#     `phase rainbow`;
#   * two FILL PANELS just outside the frame that exist only to be reflected: with the room
#     gone, a polished metal's rim points at an empty black sky and renders black.
#
# --- Why the sun is where it is -----------------------------------------------------
# None of this is eyeballed; scraps/bowgeom.py + scraps/bowmap.py solved for it. A bow is a
# circle of angular radius 42 deg about the ANTISOLAR direction, which from the camera is
# just the sun's direction of travel — so the sun alone decides where the bow lands in
# frame, and the only free choices are how much of that circle has rain on it and whether
# the backdrop behind it is dark.
#
# Scanning the whole sky against this camera picked sun ELEVATION 45, AZIMUTH -12, which
# puts a 60-degree limb of the primary sweeping from top-centre down to the right edge,
# with the secondary outside it and Alexander's dark band between. The old roofed edition
# then had to cut a slot to keep the wall behind that limb in shadow, because a bow is a
# faint single-scatter signal that vanishes against a sunlit backdrop. Opening the room up
# solved that constraint outright rather than tightening it: THERE IS NO BACKDROP ANY MORE.
# Behind the bow is black sky and a ground plane whose lit component is ~5% albedo, which is
# a darker backdrop than any shaded wall was, so the whole limb now reads.
#
# --- Why the rain is 300 um with supernumeraries OFF --------------------------------
# The first version asked for `droplet_um 500  ` with the default supernumerary train and the
# bow came out as a WIDE WHITE ARC with only a thin coloured fringe on its rim. That is the
# model being right about the wrong thing. scraps/bowplot.py reproduces rainbow.h's table in
# Python and measures it: at 500 um the coloured Airy band is genuinely only ~1.2 deg wide
# (primary at 138.76 deg for 450 nm vs 137.65 deg for 650 nm), while the MONODISPERSE
# supernumerary train rings on for another ~11 deg inside it, and eleven degrees of ringing
# read as white glare. Real rain is polydisperse, so those higher-order maxima land at
# different angles per drop size and average away; only the principal Airy lobe survives.
# `supernumerary off` is that average, and 300 um widens the surviving lobe by 1.4x
# (K scales as a^(2/3)), which also puts ~8 table bins across it instead of ~5 — the phase
# table is 2048 bins UNIFORM IN MU, so a big drop is genuinely under-resolved (logged in
# known-issues.md). Result: one clean coloured band, no white core.
#
# --- Why the sun is a real `light sun` (it used to be a distant sphere) ---------------
# Mode D is the only mode that renders BOTH this scene's bounded heterogeneous media and
# its directly-seen glass and chrome. (Forward A/B/C do the media but leave a directly-seen
# dielectric black, which is most of this room; R and the P composite shade the glass but
# flatten every bounded medium into one global homogeneous haze; see README "Modes".) So
# whatever the sun is, mode D has to accept it — and when this scene was written it did not
# accept `light sun`: a delta light was not connectible in BDPT at all, and after v0.124.0
# made it connectible on the CPU the GPU BDPT kernels still rejected it, which would have
# knocked this (very GPU-hungry) scene onto the CPU. So the sun was written as what the real
# sun also is: a Lambertian sphere of radius 1.85 m at 400 m, subtending the sun's own
# 0.53 deg, with power 1000 W/m2 * 4*pi*400^2 = 2.011e9 W.
#
# v0.126.0/v0.127.0 ported the delta-light path to the GPU BDPT and VCM kernels, so that
# constraint is gone, and the sphere turned out to be costing real image quality:
#
#   * LIGHT SELECTION — the big one. NEE and light-subpath starts both draw from a power
#     CDF, and a sphere that has to push 1000 W/m2 across 400 m needs 2.011e9 W of it.
#     Against the xenon lamp's 15 kW and the sky panel's 8 kW that is p(lamp) = 7.5e-6: the
#     lamp was sampled once in ~134,000 draws, each carrying 134,000x weight. Unbiased and
#     unusable — the lamp's light arrives as rare fireflies that clip instead of as an image.
#     MEASURED (640x360, 150 s, mode D, both absolute-exposed): the whole frame renders
#     2.1x DARKER with the sphere, and that gap collapses to 1.10x the moment the lamp is
#     deleted from both variants, i.e. the missing factor of two IS the starved lamp.
#     `light sun`'s power is irradiance * pi * R_scene^2, which is ~3.3e6 W at this
#     edition's ~33 m scene radius and leaves the sky panel a workable ~1.5%.
#   * SCENE RADIUS. A sphere 400 m out is geometry, so it dragged the scene bounding sphere
#     from ~12 m to ~400 m, and every sceneRadius-scaled quantity (env/sun phase-space
#     weights, forward-mode emission discs, BVH extent) with it.
#   * PARALLAX. 400 m is far, not infinite: the sun direction swung 0.4 deg across the rain
#     volume. That is inside the bow's ~2 deg width so it never showed, but a real `light
#     sun` is exactly parallel, which is what the 42 deg construction actually assumes.
#   * (And, until v0.128.0, a plain ENGINE BUG on top: the fp32 device build's ray/sphere
#     discriminant cancelled catastrophically at 400 m and its connection rays were
#     shortened by an absolute epsilon smaller than one float ulp of that distance, so mode
#     D self-occluded most of this sphere's shadow rays and rendered it 2.7x too dim. That
#     is fixed and is no longer an argument either way — but it is why this scene's older
#     renders are darker than the numbers here predict.)
#
# Nothing about the geometry solve changed — same direction, same 0.53 deg disc, same one
# solar constant. See "(2) the SUN" below for the irradiance arithmetic.
#
# --- Why the xenon lamp is GONE (it used to hang from the ceiling) --------------------
# The roofed edition kept a 15 kW short-arc lamp because back-projecting each hero piece
# along the sun direction showed the ceiling shaft reached only gyroid, glass, klein, brass
# and oiljack: the diamond gyroid, the dumbbell, the chrome ring, the heart and the cloud
# fell outside the slot and went black without it. Removing the roof removes that job — the
# sun now reaches every piece and the cloud directly — and leaves the lamp hanging from a
# cord attached to nothing. Two further reasons not to keep it floating:
#
#   * LIGHT SELECTION again. Opening the scene out to a ~33 m ground plane raises R_scene
#     from ~12 m to ~33 m, and the sun's power goes as R^2, so it grows ~7x to ~3.3e6 W.
#     A 15 kW lamp against that is p = 0.46% of the CDF while still supplying ~20% of what
#     the camera sees — a 40x under-sample, i.e. exactly the firefly regime the distant-sphere
#     sun used to put it in. The sky panel below is the one fill that survives that arithmetic,
#     because it is big enough (50 kW) to stay a real share.
#   * The lamp's arc emitter sits inside a nested quartz/xenon dielectric, so NEE cannot reach
#     it at all (mode W flags this); its contribution only ever arrived through forward or
#     specular-chain paths.
#
# Render (still) — don't pass -mode, the scene's prefer{}/else{} picks it:
#   ftrace -in scenes/gallery_rain.ftsl -camera cam -noise 2 \
#          -window -keepwindow -interval 15 -checkpoint -o png/gallery_rain.png
# Budget honestly: this is BDPT through three stacked media, so it is slow. Measured on an
# RTX 4090, 1280x720: 310 spp in 600 s and still at 5.7% noise, and noise falls as 1/sqrt,
# so `-noise 2` is ~2500 spp, i.e. well over an hour. `-time 600` already reads clearly (the
# bow, the cloud and the shaft are all legible); -checkpoint + -resume adds to it later.
# The flyby lives in gallery_settled.ftsl; this edition is a still, so it carries no curve.

scene { units meters  spectral 360 830 1 }

# The Klein bottle is a 2.4 mm-walled glass shell with ANOTHER TUBE INSIDE IT, so one line
# of sight through the bulb crosses about eight dielectric interfaces. Mode D runs 8 path
# edges by default, which is exactly not enough: the paths that would carry the inner tube
# run out and it renders as a solid black plug in an otherwise clear bottle. 32 is comfortably
# past the deepest stack in the room (measured: at 32 the plug is gone and the tube reads as
# glass). Cost is ~35% of the sample rate. A CLI -max-bounce still overrides this.
render { max_bounce 32 }

# --- The ground plane ----------------------------------------------------------
# Two patterns over the SAME world-space field, one per slot.
#
# `grid_ground` is the wireframe itself: distance to the nearest integer gridline in x or z,
# thresholded into a 1.8 cm core with a soft 5 cm shoulder so the line antialiases instead
# of crawling. Those are exactly half the original widths, matching the stand cages' 22 -> 11
# mm; the SHOULDER is halved along with the core on purpose, because a thin core inside a wide
# shoulder is not a thin line, it is a thin line wearing a fat halo. It drives `emit_map`, i.e.
# it METERS a green emission spectrum — the lines
# GLOW rather than being lit, which is what makes them read at all against a black sky and
# what keeps them readable in the parts of the plane the sun never reaches.
#
# `ground_fade` is the same radial falloff without the line mask, bound to `reflect_map`, so
# the plane's *reflective* component dies out at the same distance the grid does. That is
# what hides the quad's own edge: past ~22 m the ground stops returning any light at all and
# simply becomes the black sky, so there is no lit rectangle ending in mid-air and no
# rectangular shadow cast by the sky panel to give the trick away. It also kills the
# sub-pixel shimmer a centimetre-wide line would otherwise produce at 30+ m — which is why
# the fade radii were LEFT ALONE when the lines were halved: a thinner line needs that fade
# more, not less.
#
# Both fades are measured from (5, ., 3.5), the centre of the exhibit spread.
pattern "grid_ground" {
    expr "max(1 - smoothstep(0.009, 0.025, abs(fract(x + 0.5) - 0.5)),
              1 - smoothstep(0.009, 0.025, abs(fract(z + 0.5) - 0.5)))
          * (1 - smoothstep(13, 23, sqrt((x - 5)*(x - 5) + (z - 3.5)*(z - 3.5))))"
}
pattern "ground_fade" {
    expr "1 - smoothstep(10, 22, sqrt((x - 5)*(x - 5) + (z - 3.5)*(z - 3.5)))"
}
# ~5% albedo: dark enough that the sunlit plane sits well below the exhibits, bright enough
# that it still reads as a surface and still catches caustics in the forward modes. The green
# is a 528 nm Gaussian rather than an `rgb` triple because emission wants a real radiance
# magnitude — amp is spectral radiance per nm, and 0.55 puts the line core just at the clip
# point of the camera's -2 stop absolute exposure, so the lines have a blown core and a graded
# edge, which is what a glowing wire looks like.
#
# `type diffuse`, NOT glossy, and that is deliberate. The backward tracer treats Glossy as a
# purely specular lobe: `bkInteract`'s D_GLOSSY case reflects the ray and returns, with no NEE
# against the lights at all (render_cuda.cu ~6733 / backward.h). A glossy surface therefore
# shows only what its mirror direction happens to hit, and under a black sky with one small
# panel that is nothing — a glossy ground renders BLACK in modes R/W. (Verified: see
# scraps/_grid_test.ftsl, whose two glossy tiles are pure black next to two identical diffuse
# ones.) The forward modes would shade it fine, but a ground that is invisible in every
# preview is not worth a wet sheen, so the body is Lambertian and the "wet" read comes from
# the glowing lines instead.
material "gridground" {
    type diffuse
    reflect rgb 0.05 0.055 0.05
    reflect_map pattern:ground_fade
    emit gaussian 528 34 0.55
    emit_map pattern:grid_ground
}

# --- Museum-stand materials ----------------------------------------------------
# The cage bars are the ground's grid stood upright: the same green glow, on a nearly black
# body so they never read as lit plastic.
#
# The caps are the caustic SCREEN, and 0.15 — not 0.88 — is what makes them one. This is the
# single most important number in the scene and it is counter-intuitive, so: a caustic is only
# ever visible as a RATIO to the surface it lands on, and a display can only show ratios below
# its clip point. At 0.88 albedo, direct sun put these caps at 189/255 with a tenth of their
# pixels already at pure white; a 5x caustic on top of that is 945/255, and so is a 50x one,
# and so is the unfocused sunlight beside it — every one of them prints as the same #FFFFFF.
# The caps were not too dim to show a caustic, they were metered INTO THE CLIP, which erases
# contrast in exactly the way a blown highlight erases cloud detail in a photograph.
#
# 0.30 WAS THE FIRST FIX AND IT WAS NOT ENOUGH — it was set before the scene had an exhibit
# whose caustic is genuinely strong. It leaves the lit cap at 0.30 linear (sRGB 142) and so
# 3.3x of headroom, and the crystal axicon's cusps peak 6.6x its cap: 3.06% of that cap's
# pixels came back pure #FFFFFF, which is HALF the caustic's own area. The scene was still
# throwing away the colour of its one colour-bearing piece.
#
# 0.15 is one full stop lower: the lit cap sits at sRGB ~103, still an unambiguous mid grey,
# and 6.7x of headroom cuts the clipped area to 0.70% — a tenth of the caustic instead of half
# (both measured on the same 1036-spp buffer, so this is the albedo and nothing else). Darker
# still keeps helping but with sharp diminishing returns (0.09 albedo buys only +0.017 more
# spread for a visibly dingy sRGB-80 tabletop), so one stop is where this stops.
#
# WHAT ALBEDO DOES AND DOESN'T MOVE, because it is easy to measure this wrong: on the linear
# render it changes NOTHING — chromaticity and peak:median are both scale-invariant, so spread,
# sat and coverage come back identical to three decimals at 0.15 and at 0.30. The albedo is
# purely a TONE-MAP decision: what it buys is caustic that survives the clamp. Metered through
# that clamp, i.e. as a viewer sees it, the caustic goes from spread 0.111 to 0.147 (+32%) and
# sat 0.167 to 0.228.
#
# HOW TO RE-DERIVE THIS after any change to the sun, the exposure or the pieces: render with
# `-hdr -fireflies 3` and run `python scraps/_capchroma.py <out>.pfm`, which meters each cap in
# the finished frame and reports its clipped fraction. Never meter the PNG — it is 8-bit sRGB,
# a caustic is by definition the brightest thing in frame, and at the clip point all three
# channels are equal, so the tone map deletes exactly the hue and peak ratio being measured
# (the PNG reported this caustic at spread 0.057 / peak 3.7x against the linear buffer's
# 0.19 / 6.6x). `-fireflies 3` is not optional either: the clamp used to flatten mode D's
# rare monochromatic spikes to white and was, by accident, the only outlier rejection this
# scene's measurements had.
#
# The cost is real and was accepted deliberately: the tabletops are not brilliant white. A
# gallery plinth in raking sun is a mid grey anyway; brilliant white was the render clipping.
material "wirecage" { type diffuse  reflect rgb 0.02 0.05 0.03
                      emit gaussian 528 34 0.45 }
material "capwhite" { type diffuse  reflect whitewall 0.15 }   # the caps' pre-marble albedo
material "ceil"     { type diffuse  reflect whitewall 0.82 }   # raincloud stand-in only

# --- MARBLE TABLETOPS ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every cap is a different slab of marble. The images are prepared by
# `python tools/make_marble_caps.py` (sources: the repo-root `marble texture *` drops) and
# written to `textures/`; that script is where the reasoning and the per-cap knobs live.
#
# THE ONE THING THAT MATTERS HERE: a cap is the caustic SCREEN, and every raw marble photo
# means 0.34-0.85 LINEAR against the 0.15 that `capwhite` above spends forty lines
# justifying. Dropping one in unprepared raises a cap's albedo up to 5.7x — two and a half
# stops back INTO the clip the 0.15 exists to escape — and every caustic in the scene goes
# back to printing as flat #FFFFFF. So each image is rescaled at prep time to a MEAN LINEAR
# REFLECTANCE of exactly 0.15. The stone's pattern survives; its brightness does not. The
# caps are the same mid grey they were, now with veins.
#
# Two further preparations, because a caustic on a diffuse surface is MULTIPLICATIVE
# (pixel = albedo(x) * irradiance(x)), so the stone modulates the caustic directly rather
# than sitting behind it:
#   * CONTRAST is compressed on the three caps that catch a measured caustic, so a dark vein
#     cannot erase the caustic crossing it. Those three end up swinging only 1.29-1.37x
#     peak-to-trough (p2..p98), i.e. +-15% on the caustic, against 6.0-8.2x left on the caps
#     that catch nothing.
#   * SATURATION is pulled toward neutral on those same three, because a coloured stone TINTS
#     the caustic — which attacks the exact quantity (`sat`, `spread`) the axicon was recut in
#     flint to produce. The axicon's own slab is the flattest and most neutral sheet in the
#     set (source chroma 0.003) and is further desaturated to 0.30 of that.
#
# WHICH SLAB GOES WHERE IS BY MEASURED CAUSTIC STRENGTH, not by taste. Only three pieces put
# anything on their cap (design.md's float ranking): the axicon (the only piece that makes
# COLOUR), the crystal orb, and the solid crystal gyroid. The other seven are opaque, metal,
# or — for the Klein bottle — optically a window, so their caps are free and get the dramatic
# gold-veined stone. That is also why the GOLD gyroid and the BRASS cluster stand on the
# gold-veined slabs: the vein colour rhymes with the metal at zero caustic cost.
texture "tex_marble_gold"     { file textures/marble_gold.png     encoding srgb  filter bilinear  wrap clamp }
texture "tex_marble_orb"      { file textures/marble_orb.png      encoding srgb  filter bilinear  wrap clamp }
texture "tex_marble_gyroidx"  { file textures/marble_gyroidx.png  encoding srgb  filter bilinear  wrap clamp }
texture "tex_marble_klein"    { file textures/marble_klein.png    encoding srgb  filter bilinear  wrap clamp }
texture "tex_marble_dumbbell" { file textures/marble_dumbbell.png encoding srgb  filter bilinear  wrap clamp }
texture "tex_marble_chrome"   { file textures/marble_chrome.png   encoding srgb  filter bilinear  wrap clamp }
texture "tex_marble_heart"    { file textures/marble_heart.png    encoding srgb  filter bilinear  wrap clamp }
texture "tex_marble_brass"    { file textures/marble_brass.png    encoding srgb  filter bilinear  wrap clamp }
texture "tex_marble_oil"      { file textures/marble_oil.png      encoding srgb  filter bilinear  wrap clamp }
texture "tex_marble_axicon"   { file textures/marble_axicon.png   encoding srgb  filter bilinear  wrap clamp }

# `wrap clamp` (not `repeat`) with `uv planar axis=y` on each cap isosurface maps ONE slab
# across each tabletop, referenced to that primitive's own world bounds — a quarried slab cut
# to the table, not a tiled bathroom floor. The projection is down the y axis, so the top face
# (the only one that catches anything, and nearly the only one visible from the still camera)
# carries the image undistorted; the 0.20-0.24 m rims inherit the same projection, which is
# what a real slab's edge does anyway.
material "capmarble_gold"     { type diffuse  reflect texture:tex_marble_gold }
material "capmarble_orb"      { type diffuse  reflect texture:tex_marble_orb }
material "capmarble_gyroidx"  { type diffuse  reflect texture:tex_marble_gyroidx }
material "capmarble_klein"    { type diffuse  reflect texture:tex_marble_klein }
material "capmarble_dumbbell" { type diffuse  reflect texture:tex_marble_dumbbell }
material "capmarble_chrome"   { type diffuse  reflect texture:tex_marble_chrome }
material "capmarble_heart"    { type diffuse  reflect texture:tex_marble_heart }
material "capmarble_brass"    { type diffuse  reflect texture:tex_marble_brass }
material "capmarble_oil"      { type diffuse  reflect texture:tex_marble_oil }
material "capmarble_axicon"   { type diffuse  reflect texture:tex_marble_axicon }

# --- Hero-object materials -----------------------------------------------------
# `roughness` after `preset` overrides the preset's polished lobe. Gold needs a LOT here, and
# for two opposite reasons that pull the same way:
#   * TOO BRIGHT in the middle. A perfect mirror pointed at a 1000 W/m2 sun returns a
#     highlight ~2.4x the clip point in EVERY channel (gold's normal-incidence reflectance is
#     ~0.95/0.86/0.42), so the front of the gyroid rendered as flat white — "grey gold".
#   * TOO DARK at the rim. A mirror has no colour of its own; it shows what it points AT, and
#     with the walls and ceiling gone the gyroid's outer edge points at an empty black sky and
#     so renders black. Roughness is the only fix that does not require putting the room back:
#     a wide lobe lets a rim microfacet still collect the sun, the overhead sky panel and the
#     white caps, and gold's spectral reflectance then tints all of it gold.
# 0.18 is the value that spreads the central spike below the clip point AND leaves the rim
# lit. It reads as satin/brushed gold rather than polished — which is what a wide lobe IS.
material "gold"     { preset gold  roughness 0.18 }
material "chrome"   { preset chrome }
material "brass"    { preset brass }
# CRYSTAL, not BK7, and the reason is COLOUR IN THE CAUSTICS. What splays a caustic across
# the spectrum is not the index, it is the DISPERSION, measured by the Abbe number
# V_d = (n_d-1)/(n_F-n_C) — LOWER means more colour. From the Sellmeier coefficients:
#
#     glass       n(486)   n(589)   n(656)   n_F-n_C    V_d    f/R of a solid sphere
#     BK7          1.5224   1.5167   1.5143   0.00806   64.1        1.468
#     crystal(F2)  1.6321   1.6199   1.6150   0.01706   36.3        1.307
#     SF10         1.7464   1.7281   1.7209   0.02553   28.5        1.187
#     diamond      2.4355   2.4173   2.4099   0.02564   55.3        0.853
#
# Crystal splays 2.1x as far across the spectrum as BK7 — the difference between a white
# blob with a faint coloured rim and a caustic with colour actually in it. It is also the
# honest material for this room: BK7 is an optical blank, cut lead crystal is what a
# gallery would put on a plinth. `glass:SF10` splays 3.2x if more is wanted.
material "glass"    { type dielectric ior glass:crystal }
#
# AND MORE IS WANTED, BY EXACTLY ONE PIECE. Crystal is right for the orb and the gyroid,
# whose caustics are white-with-a-rim whatever the glass, but the axicon exists ONLY to put
# colour on a screen, so it is cut from dense flint instead. Head to head in the isolation
# rig at matched settings (`_gemsweep.py` at GEMRES=960 GEMSPP=1200, each at its own best
# drop — a denser glass deviates harder, so it moves the ring focus and the drop has to be
# re-optimised with it, 0.35 -> 0.65):
#
#     glass    drop   peak   coverage   sat     spread   fan    patch
#     crystal  0.35   16.4x   0.28%     0.321   0.152    0.51   1.37 x 0.98
#     SF10     0.50   24.7x   0.13%     0.509   0.162    0.57   1.27 x 1.05
#     SF10     0.65   22.0x   0.15%     0.516   0.187    0.77   1.41 x 1.14
#
# SF10 at 0.65 wins on every colour axis at the same patch size — 1.6x the saturation, 1.2x
# the chromatic spread, 1.5x the `fan` (how much of the colour is a smooth FUNCTION OF
# POSITION rather than sampler speckle) — and pays for it in coverage, 0.15% against 0.28%.
# That is the right trade here: the axicon's cusps are already the largest caustic in the
# scene by an order of magnitude (5.96% of its cap in the finished frame), so area is the
# one thing it does not need more of, and half of a lot is still far more than any other
# piece has. Colour was the whole reason the exhibit exists.
material "flint"    { type dielectric ior glass:SF10 }
#
# `diamond` is NOT the caustic material, despite dispersing as hard as SF10. A solid sphere
# focuses at f = n/(2(n-1)) times its radius FROM ITS CENTRE, and at n=2.42 that is 0.853 R
# — INSIDE the glass. A diamond ball cannot form an external focus at all: it total-
# internally-reflects and SPARKLES instead. Crystal's 1.307 R puts the focus 0.31 R clear of
# the surface, close enough to concentrate hard and far enough to land on a cap. Kept as a
# named material because sparkle is a real look, but nothing in this scene uses it now.
material "diamond"  { preset diamond }
material "morpho"   { preset morpho }       # iridescent multilayer Bragg (blue morpho)
material "oilslick" { preset oil-slick }    # iridescent thin-film (rainbow) — 2nd iridescent, different model

# --- THE GROUND. One quad, 46 x 45 m, x[-18,28] z[-19,26] — no walls, no ceiling.
# The exhibits used to occupy the old hall's footprint (x in [3.2, 6.8], z in [1.5, 4.8]); the
# 1.6x SPREAD below opened that to caps spanning x [1.90, 8.25] and z [0.34, 7.10], and the
# crystal axicon then extended it to x=1.80. `scraps/_standaudit.py -v` prints the current
# footprints — read them off it rather than off this comment before siting anything new.
#
# The extent is a compromise, not a "make it huge". `light sun`'s power is
# irradiance * pi * R_scene^2, and R_scene is set by the geometry, so every metre of ground
# quadratically inflates the sun's share of the power CDF that NEE and light-subpath starts
# draw from — which is what starves every other light in the scene (see the header). 46 x 45
# puts R_scene at ~33 m, a 7x growth that the remaining sky panel can absorb. The plane still
# reads as unbounded because `ground_fade` extinguishes it ~11 m INSIDE its own edge, well
# before the horizon: what the camera sees is a ground that dissolves into the black sky,
# not a rectangle floating in space.
#
# WINDING MATTERS HERE. A quad's geometric normal is cross(u, v), and the backward tracer
# only adds a surface's own emission on a camera/specular arrival that hits the FRONT face
# (`specularArrival && dot(rd, ng) < 0`, render_cuda.cu ~7006). With `u 46 0 0  v 0 0 45`
# cross(u,v) is -y, the plane faces the floor, and the grid is invisible from above even
# though the diffuse body shades normally via NEE (which flips the normal itself). Swapping
# u and v puts cross(u,v) at +y and the lines light up. The old stone floor never noticed
# because it had no emission.
quad { origin -18 0 -19   u 0 0 45   v 46 0 0   material gridground }

# ==============================  MUSEUM STANDS  ================================
# Every stand is now TWO objects at the same place:
#
#   `stand_<piece>`      the green wireframe CAGE — the old base slab and column, hollowed
#                        out to their twelve edges;
#   `stand_<piece>_cap`  the SOLID WHITE CAP the hero piece rests on.
#
# Splitting them is forced by materials (one isosurface carries one material) and is the
# whole point of the design: a cage catches essentially no light, so if the cap were wireframe
# too the dispersive caustics thrown by the glass sphere and the diamond gyroid would land on
# nothing and simply vanish from the image. The cap is a mid-grey 0.15 rather than a bright
# white, which is the OPPOSITE of the obvious choice and is explained at `capwhite`: a bright
# cap in this sun clips, and a clipped screen cannot show a caustic at any intensity. Both
# halves are NAMED, because settle_scene.py
# makes every named object a static collider and the pieces must land on the cap, not fall
# through it.
#
# A CAGE IS A CSG DIFFERENCE, not twelve hand-placed bars: take the original solid box and
# subtract three axis-aligned boxes, each shrunk by the bar thickness on two axes and grown
# past the surface on the third. What survives is exactly the twelve edges. Bar thickness is
# 0.011 m (2 * 0.011 = 0.022 comes off each shrunk axis) and the subtractors overhang by
# 0.02 m so the cut faces are clean rather than coplanar. Four primitives instead of twelve,
# and every one an exact box SDF, so the sphere tracer stays well-behaved even on 11 mm bars.
#
# The CAPS are deliberately much wider than the cage that carries them — a cap is the only
# surface in the scene that can actually catch a caustic, so widening it is the cheapest way
# to see more of the glass pieces' dispersion. Several cap pairs overlap in PLAN, which is
# fine because each sits at a different height and they stagger like shelves.
#
# What is NOT fine, and was the bug the widening introduced, is a cap overlapping a
# neighbouring stand's COLUMN: a column spans a whole range of y, so plan overlap plus any
# height at all is a real intersection. `scraps/_standaudit.py` checks every cage box and
# every cap box against every other stand's, in 3-D, and must come back clean. (It also
# found two base-slab intersections that predated the wireframe rework — see the notes on
# stand_glass and stand_brass, both now moved.)
#
# CAP THICKNESS = THAT STAND'S OWN BASE SLAB (0.18–0.24 m), not the 0.03 m the stone version
# used. At 3 cm a cap read as a sheet of paper laid over a wire frame while the slab under the
# same stand was eight times thicker — the two ends of one object disagreeing about what it is
# made of. Matching each cap to its own slab makes the stand read as one piece of furniture,
# and it is per-stand rather than one global number precisely so each stand stays internally
# consistent (the gyroid plinth is chunky at 0.24, the orb's light pedestal 0.18).
#
# THE EXTRA THICKNESS IS ADDED DOWNWARD, i.e. every cap's TOP FACE y is unchanged. That is
# not cosmetic bookkeeping: the hero pieces were dropped onto these caps by
# `scraps/settle_scene.py` and their rest poses are recorded as literal coordinates, so
# moving a top face by even a centimetre would leave a piece floating or sunk. Only the
# bottom face moved, and the bottom face touches nothing. (One consequence to know: on the
# gyroid plinth, whose middle cage tier is only 0.28 m tall, a 0.24 m cap now covers all but
# ~7 cm of it, so that stand reads as two slabs with a wire gap between them rather than
# three tiers. Deliberate — it is the centrepiece and mostly hidden behind the gold ball.)
#
# Cap `round` and the two stands that MOVED (glass, brass) are unchanged from the stone
# version; those took their hero piece with them by the same offset, so nothing needs
# re-settling there either.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE SPREAD.  Every exhibit — its cage, its cap, its hero piece and any mount — is wrapped
# in a `group { translate dx 0 dz }` that pushes it radially OUTWARD from the layout centre
# (5.00, ., 2.72), the centroid of the nine original pedestal axes.  The displacement is
# 0.6 * (p - centre), i.e. a 1.6x RADIAL SPREAD, rounded to 5 cm:
#
#     exhibit    was (x,z)      offset          now (x,z)
#     gyroid     5.00 4.80    ( 0.00, +1.25)   5.00 6.05
#     gem        3.50 3.60    (-0.90, +0.55)   2.60 4.15
#     glass      6.70 3.50    (+1.00, +0.45)   7.70 3.95
#     klein      5.90 2.60    (+0.55, -0.05)   6.45 2.55
#     dumbbell   4.50 2.70    (-0.30,  0.00)   4.20 2.70
#     chrome     3.50 2.00    (-0.90, -0.45)   2.60 1.55
#     heart      4.60 1.90    (-0.25, -0.50)   4.35 1.40
#     brass      6.00 1.84    (+0.60, -0.54)   6.60 1.30
#     oil        5.30 1.50    (+0.20, -0.75)   5.50 0.75
#
# The x span goes 3.20 -> 5.10 m and the z span 3.30 -> 5.30 m while every piece keeps its
# own size, which is what "farther apart" means: the pieces did not grow, the gaps did.
# There are no walls to run into any more, so the only cost is that the still camera has to
# back off by the same 1.6x (see the CAMERAS section) and each piece therefore reads 1.6x
# smaller in the frame.  That is paid for twice over by what the room buys:
#   * every cap is now clear of its neighbours' columns, so a cap can finally be made WIDE
#     enough to catch a whole caustic (the gem and klein caps below are, and could not have
#     been at the old spacing);
#   * the flyby has room to bank between pieces instead of threading a corridor.
#
# WHY A GROUP AND NOT REWRITTEN COORDINATES.  Every number inside a stand block, and every
# comment that quotes one, is authored in the ORIGINAL layout frame and stays exactly true
# there; the group is the single place the new position lives.  Relationships WITHIN one
# exhibit (cap over column, pin against sphere, settled rest pose) are untouched by a rigid
# translation, so nothing needs re-deriving and nothing needs re-settling.  The one class of
# comment the spread does stale is a clearance quoted BETWEEN two different exhibits (they
# move by different offsets) — every one of those gaps only ever GREW, so they read as
# conservative rather than wrong, and `scraps/_standaudit.py` is what actually proves the
# scene collision-free after any change here.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

group { translate 0.00 0 1.25          # --- exhibit: GOLD GYROID ---
isosurface "stand_gyroid" {   # centrepiece: broad low plinth, cap top y=0.55
    material wirecage  union {
        difference {
            box { center 5.0 0.12 4.8  size 2.1   0.24  2.1   }
            box { center 5.0 0.12 4.8  size 2.12  0.218 2.078 }
            box { center 5.0 0.12 4.8  size 2.078 0.26  2.078 }
            box { center 5.0 0.12 4.8  size 2.078 0.218 2.12  }
        }
        difference {
            box { center 5.0 0.38 4.8  size 1.66  0.28  1.66  }
            box { center 5.0 0.38 4.8  size 1.68  0.258 1.638 }
            box { center 5.0 0.38 4.8  size 1.638 0.3   1.638 }
            box { center 5.0 0.38 4.8  size 1.638 0.258 1.68  }
        }
    }
}
isosurface "stand_gyroid_cap" { material capmarble_gold
    uv planar axis=y
    # 2.3 wide but only 2.1 DEEP. At 2.3 deep the cap's near edge sat at z=3.65 and sliced
    # straight through the glass and diamond stands' COLUMNS (which reach z=3.71 and 3.72,
    # and span y=0.18..0.87 and 0.19..0.81 — both straddling the cap's own y=0.52..0.55).
    # 2.1 pulls the near edge back to 3.75 and clears both, for 9% of the catching area.
    box { center 5.0 0.43 4.8  size 2.3 0.24 2.1  round 0.01 } }
}

group { translate 1.00 0 0.45          # --- exhibit: GLASS ORB ---
isosurface "stand_glass" {   # right-front pedestal, cap top y=0.90
    # Moved from x=6.2 to x=6.7. At 6.2 its base slab (x 5.75..6.65) drove 0.30 m into the
    # gyroid plinth's base slab (x 3.95..6.05) — a pre-existing intersection from the Tron
    # rework, two wireframe boxes crossing through each other at floor level. 6.7 puts the
    # base at x 6.25..7.15, clear of the plinth (6.05) and of the klein stand (6.26), and it
    # buys the room the raised orb's caustic needs on the near (-z) side of the cap.
    material wirecage  union {
        difference {
            box { center 6.7 0.09 3.5  size 0.9   0.18  0.9   }
            box { center 6.7 0.09 3.5  size 0.92  0.158 0.878 }
            box { center 6.7 0.09 3.5  size 0.878 0.2   0.878 }
            box { center 6.7 0.09 3.5  size 0.878 0.158 0.92  }
        }
        difference {
            box { center 6.7 0.525 3.5  size 0.42  0.69  0.42  }
            box { center 6.7 0.525 3.5  size 0.44  0.668 0.398 }
            box { center 6.7 0.525 3.5  size 0.398 0.71  0.398 }
            box { center 6.7 0.525 3.5  size 0.398 0.668 0.44  }
        }
    }
}
isosurface "stand_glass_cap" { material capmarble_orb
    uv planar axis=y
    # One of the two CANTILEVERED caps (the crystal gyroid's is the other, and for exactly
    # the same reason). 1.7 deep and centred at z=3.05, i.e. 0.45 m
    # toward the camera-far side of its column at z=3.5. That is not decoration — it is
    # where the orb's caustic actually lands. Sunlight travels (0.147, -0.707, -0.692), i.e.
    # +0.2079 x and -0.9781 z per metre of drop, so over the 0.80 m from the orb's centre
    # (y=1.70) to this cap (y=0.90) the focal axis walks +0.17 m in x and -0.78 m in z,
    # putting the disc at (6.87, 2.72). A cap centred under the column would have missed it
    # by half a metre; even this one only just contains it (near edge z=2.20).
    box { center 6.7 0.81 3.05  size 1.1 0.18 1.7  round 0.02 } }
}

group { translate -0.90 0 0.55         # --- exhibit: CRYSTAL GYROID ---
isosurface "stand_diamond" {   # tall thin jewel-column, cap top y=0.85
    material wirecage  union {
        difference {
            box { center 3.5 0.1 3.6  size 0.6   0.2   0.6   }
            box { center 3.5 0.1 3.6  size 0.62  0.178 0.578 }
            box { center 3.5 0.1 3.6  size 0.578 0.22  0.578 }
            box { center 3.5 0.1 3.6  size 0.578 0.178 0.62  }
        }
        difference {
            box { center 3.5 0.5 3.6  size 0.24  0.62  0.24  }
            box { center 3.5 0.5 3.6  size 0.26  0.598 0.218 }
            box { center 3.5 0.5 3.6  size 0.218 0.64  0.218 }
            box { center 3.5 0.5 3.6  size 0.218 0.598 0.26  }
        }
    }
}
isosurface "stand_diamond_cap" { material capmarble_gyroidx
    uv planar axis=y
    # The second CANTILEVERED cap, and the bigger one. Raising the gyroid to y=1.75 (see the
    # piece below) puts 0.90 m of drop between it and this cap top at y=0.85, and the sun
    # walks the focal axis +0.2079 x / -0.9788 z per metre of that drop -- so the caustic
    # core lands at (3.5+0.187, 3.6-0.881) = (3.687, 2.719), most of a metre off the column.
    # 1.7 x 1.8 centred at (3.65, 2.95) spans x 2.80..4.50, z 2.05..3.85: the core sits well
    # inside, and the outer filigree the solid network throws has room to fall on white
    # rather than off the edge into the floor cage. In world coordinates (this exhibit is
    # displaced -0.90, +0.55 by the spread) that is x 1.90..3.60, z 2.60..4.40.
    box { center 3.65 0.75 2.95  size 1.7 0.20 1.8  round 0.02 } }
}

group { translate 0.55 0 -0.05         # --- exhibit: KLEIN BOTTLE ---
isosurface "stand_klein" {   # cap top y=1.00; the Klein bottle stands on it
    material wirecage  union {
        difference {
            box { center 5.9 0.11 2.6  size 0.72  0.22  0.72  }
            box { center 5.9 0.11 2.6  size 0.74  0.198 0.698 }
            box { center 5.9 0.11 2.6  size 0.698 0.24  0.698 }
            box { center 5.9 0.11 2.6  size 0.698 0.198 0.74  }
        }
        difference {
            box { center 5.9 0.6 2.6  size 0.34  0.76  0.34  }
            box { center 5.9 0.6 2.6  size 0.36  0.738 0.318 }
            box { center 5.9 0.6 2.6  size 0.318 0.78  0.318 }
            box { center 5.9 0.6 2.6  size 0.318 0.738 0.36  }
        }
    }
}
isosurface "stand_klein_cap" { material capmarble_klein
    uv planar axis=y
    # Widened, but NOT cantilevered, and deliberately so. The bottle is a 2.4 mm-walled
    # shell -- optically a window, not a lens -- and measured over a bare cap it peaks at
    # only 2.0x the surrounding white with nothing at all above 1.5x (scraps/_gemsweep.py,
    # `piece klein`). There is no focus to chase, just a soft brightening, and with barely
    # 0.30 m from the body down to the cap the sun walks it only +0.06 x / -0.29 z, landing
    # at (5.96, 2.31). So this stays centred on its column and simply gets bigger, so that
    # patch and the bottle's own shadow-and-glint both fall on white instead of running off
    # the near edge. 1.2 x 1.30 at (5.95, 2.50) = x 5.35..6.55, z 1.85..3.15; in world terms
    # (spread +0.55, -0.05) x 5.90..7.10, z 1.80..3.10 -- which is why it stops there: the
    # brass cap ends at z=1.73 and the orb's cantilever starts at x=7.15.
    box { center 5.95 0.89 2.50  size 1.2 0.22 1.30  round 0.02 } }
}

group { translate -0.30 0 0.00         # --- exhibit: BRASS DUMBBELL ---
isosurface "stand_dumbbell" {   # cap top y=0.88
    material wirecage  union {
        difference {
            box { center 4.5 0.11 2.7  size 0.66  0.22  0.66  }
            box { center 4.5 0.11 2.7  size 0.68  0.198 0.638 }
            box { center 4.5 0.11 2.7  size 0.638 0.24  0.638 }
            box { center 4.5 0.11 2.7  size 0.638 0.198 0.68  }
        }
        difference {
            box { center 4.5 0.54 2.7  size 0.34  0.66  0.34  }
            box { center 4.5 0.54 2.7  size 0.36  0.638 0.318 }
            box { center 4.5 0.54 2.7  size 0.318 0.68  0.318 }
            box { center 4.5 0.54 2.7  size 0.318 0.638 0.36  }
        }
    }
}
isosurface "stand_dumbbell_cap" { material capmarble_dumbbell
    uv planar axis=y
    box { center 4.5 0.77 2.7  size 0.88 0.22 0.88  round 0.02 } }
}

group { translate -0.90 0 -0.45        # --- exhibit: CHROME RING ---
isosurface "stand_chrome" {   # cap top y=0.90
    material wirecage  union {
